# Test: each interface of a multi-interface policy is its own counter row in
#       `show firewall ruleset`.
# VALIDATES: spec-policyroute-interface-list-matches-no-packet AC-7 -- an
#            operator who reads the ruleset back sees steer-webmark-1 and
#            steer-webmark-2, one row for each named interface, and no row
#            carrying the pair's summed packets.
# PREVENTS: the group's terms taking one name again. mergeRuleCounters
#           (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/backend_linux.go) adds the packets of
#           every rule that shares a term name into ONE TermCounter, because a
#           term split over an IPv4 and an IPv6 rule is one term to the operator.
#           Two interfaces are two terms, so one name for both would report the
#           pair's packets as the total of each row.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATING ASSERTION is the reject line. handleShowFirewallRuleset
# (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/cmd_show.go) prints one row for each APPLIED
# term and looks its counter up by name, so a group whose terms shared the base
# name prints "steer-webmark" twice, each row carrying the merged total. The two
# expect lines require the suffixed names, and the reject line refuses the
# unsuffixed one, which no correct output ever carries.
#
# The packet counts are NOT asserted, and they would not discriminate: applyChain
# prepends the counter expression BEFORE the term's matches, so every rule in the
# chain counts every packet the chain sees and both rows report the same number
# whichever interface the traffic arrived on.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE from policy-interface-list.ci, which proves the
# same policy installs two rules. `ze cli` is a foreground `ze`, and the runner
# waits for a foreground ze only when it is the LAST command. Placed before the
# ze-test fixture step, the client is left running, the fixture stops the daemon,
# and the answer never reaches the assertion buffer. So this test ends on the
# client, and the daemon is stopped by teardown.
#
# `ze cli` speaks SSH and nothing else (runBGP, internal/component/cli/client/
# main.go), so the daemon declares a server and a user, and the client takes its
# name and password from the environment. Without them it stops at "no
# credentials for 127.0.0.1:<port>" before any command is dispatched.
#
# The sleep step is a fence the runner has no primitive for. The daemon writes
# ze.ready.file (cmd/ze/hub/main.go), which the runner waits on, BEFORE the SSH
# listener accepts: an unfenced `ze cli` answered "cannot connect to daemon:
# connect to 127.0.0.1:<port>: connection refused" and the listener logged "SSH
# server listening" 200ms later. `await=stderr` cannot serve as the fence
# because it tears the daemon down.
#
# Neither interface has to exist. An iifname match compares packet metadata
# rather than looking an index up, so no option=netns-link is declared.
#
# Requires CAP_NET_ADMIN.
#
# ISOLATION: read the header of policy-boot-apply.ci. Every test in test/policy/
# writes the same `inet ze_pr` table and the same global ip-rule table, so the
# suite runs serial with a per-test namespace. Do NOT raise its parallelism.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=timeout:value=40s

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
firewall {
	backend nft;
}
policy {
	route steer {
		interface eth0;
		interface "l2tp*";
		rule webmark {
			from {
				destination-port 80,443;
				protocol tcp;
			}
			then {
				table 100;
			}
		}
	}
}
system {
	authentication {
		user operator {
			# bcrypt hash of testpass.
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO";
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true;
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# The key is DOTTED: the engine maps the registry name "policy-routes" to the
# subsystem "policy.routes" (CanonicalSubsystemName), so ze.log.policy-routes
# matches nothing. policy-boot-apply.ci carries the full explanation.
option=env:var=ze.log.policy.routes:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.firewall:value=info

# The client's credentials. ze.ssh.username stands in for the --user flag
# (resolveUsername, internal/core/ssh/client/client.go), and ze.ssh.insecure
# skips the host-key check the zefs store would otherwise answer.
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=operator
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.insecure:value=true
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT2

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=sleep 5
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "show firewall ruleset pr | json"
expect=stderr:contains=policy routes applied
expect=stdout:contains="steer-webmark-1"
expect=stdout:contains="steer-webmark-2"
reject=stdout:contains="steer-webmark"
